<section class="case-section">
  <div class="case-section__head"><span class="mono" data-reveal>Context</span><h2 class="display-s" data-split>Dice you build, a run you lose, a ladder you climb.</h2></div>
  <div class="case-section__body prose" data-reveal>
    <p>Don’t Die is a roguelike auto-battler where your build is a set of dice. Each face is a choice: keep it, burn it, upgrade it, or roll the risk. Runs are short and fatal; the meta is long. It was one of the live titles I directed at Anomaly Games, and the one that taught me the most about balancing systems with variance baked in.</p>
    <p>Dice games are honest about luck, which means the designer has to be honest about probability. A face that feels great in a playtest can quietly warp a thousand runs. You cannot feel your way to that. You have to count.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="case-section__head"><span class="mono" data-reveal>What I did</span><h2 class="display-s" data-split>Count first. Then trust the feel.</h2></div>
  <div class="case-section__body" data-reveal>
    <ul>
      <li><span><b>Simulation framework.</b> Built an MCTS- and CFR-inspired harness that plays the game thousands of times per change, surfacing dominant faces, dead faces and difficulty cliffs before a patch ships.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Dice pool design.</b> Curated the face pool and the rarity ladder so every die tells a story and no story is mandatory.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Ranked PvP.</b> Directed the ranked mode from spec to ship.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Economy &amp; progression.</b> Tuned the reward curve so a lost run still moves the meta forward.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Direction.</b> One roadmap for design, engineering and art, and reviews that kept the game’s intent legible to everyone on it.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="case-section__head"><span class="mono" data-reveal>Why it matters</span><h2 class="display-s" data-split>The method outgrew the game.</h2></div>
  <div class="case-section__body prose" data-reveal>
    <p>The simulation-first approach I built for Don’t Die became the way I work. It is the same discipline behind the 1,800-run AI playtest of SHIPLESS, and the reason I would rather find a broken die on my laptop at midnight than in a player review the next morning.</p>
  </div>
</section>

